alex: Heads up — found a weird goroutine pile-up in the Go sampleapp when auth refresh happens midstream. Repro + logs below.

alex: Repro summary: auth token rotates while a generator.Pull is active; we backoff/retry and start a new Pull but the old goroutine never returns. CPU climbs.

alex: Code we use (simplified):
```go
for {
  ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(parentCtx, 30*time.Second)
  go func() {
    // start generator Pull with ctx
    for msg := range client.Pull(ctx) {
      handle(msg)
    }
  }()
  // sometimes auth refresh happens here and we retry the loop
  cancel()
}
```

sara: which sdk version? is the sampleapp creating a new client on refresh or reusing?

alex: v0.9.1, sampleapp creates a new client after refresh (we re-init to pick up new creds).

cheng: likely leak from the goroutine capturing the child ctx. If you spin a goroutine and cancel parent, but the stream's Next/Recv blocks on network, it can hang if stream.Close()/Cancel not called.

alex: Here's a stacktrace from the repro run:
```
runtime.goexit
github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-go/gen.(*stream).Next
net/http.(*persistConn).readLoop
```

kevin: I've seen similar — when auth returns transient 401 and the app creates a new client, the old stream's handshake doesn't get signaled to close due to the way the runtime's connection pool handles cancellations. Old goroutines remain waiting on `Next()`.

alex: Workaround I tried: explicitly call stream.Close() and wait for goroutine to exit before starting a retry. That fixes the leak locally. But sampleapp isn't doing that now.

sara: quick patch for sampleapp: don't spawn anonymous goroutine per loop; instead handle stream inline and ensure `defer stream.Close()` plus select on ctx.Done(). Example patch: 
```go
stream := client.Pull(ctx)
defer stream.Close()
for {
  select {
  case <-ctx.Done():
    return ctx.Err()
  case msg, ok := <-stream.Chan():
    if !ok { return nil }
    handle(msg)
  }
}
```

cheng: SDK-side: we should document auth-refresh pattern and maybe add helper `WithStream(ctx, func(s Stream) error)` that guarantees stream closed on function exit. Also consider having stream.Next() check for parent context cancellation more eagerly.

kevin: filed an internal issue (#1423) to add a helper and improve docs — assigned to eng-ml. For immediate fix, updating the sampleapp to close streams on retry + avoid child-context goroutines should unblock onboarding. :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: created branch fix/onboard-stream-cancel-1758001111

alex: I'll open a PR today with the sampleapp change and add a short runbook entry on the auth-refresh repro steps and mitigation. Thanks all.
